7. Java connection → How does this relate to things you've already used?
You've probably encountered retries without thinking about them as a distributed-systems concept.
For example, Java HTTP clients can encounter:
ConnectTimeoutException
SocketTimeoutException
IOException
A simplistic implementation might be:
for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 3; attempt++) {
    try {
        return client.call();
    } catch (IOException e) {
        if (attempt == 3) {
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
But production code should conceptually evolve toward:
RetryPolicy policy = ...;

while (policy.canAttempt()) {

    try {
        return call();
    } catch (Exception e) {

        if (!policy.isRetryable(e)) {
            throw e;
        }

        Duration delay = policy.nextDelay();

        sleep(delay);
    }
}
Notice what has happened.
We've moved from:
"try again"
to:
Should I retry?
How many times?
When?
For which errors?
Under which deadline?
Is the operation safe to repeat?
That is the transition from Java coding to production engineering.
________________________________________
Where this appears in real Java systems
You'll encounter retry behavior around:
•	HTTP clients 
•	database clients 
•	Redis 
•	Kafka producers/consumers 
•	cloud SDKs 
•	service-to-service calls 
•	message brokers 
•	distributed locks 
•	leader election 
•	service discovery 
Libraries/frameworks can provide mechanisms for this, but I don't want you to learn:
"Use library X and configure property Y."
Instead, I want you to understand why the library needs those properties in the first place.
For example, if you later see:
maxAttempts: 3
backoff:
  initialInterval: 100ms
  multiplier: 2
you should immediately visualize:
dependency failure
      ↓
don't hammer dependency
      ↓
wait
      ↓
try again
      ↓
limit total attempts
And then ask:
"Is this operation actually safe to repeat?"
That question is more valuable than memorizing the configuration.
