Lesson 1 — Connection vs Read vs Request Timeout
1. Problem → Why did engineers need timeouts?
Start with a simple Java service:
OrderService
     |
     | HTTP
     v
PaymentService
Without a timeout, OrderService could theoretically wait forever:
OrderService
     |
     | request
     v
PaymentService
     |
     | ... never responds ...
     |
     |
     |
     v
   ?
Imagine your Java thread is sitting here:
PaymentResponse response =
    paymentClient.charge(request);
What happens if PaymentService:
•	crashes? 
•	becomes overloaded? 
•	gets stuck waiting for its database? 
•	has a network problem? 
•	receives the request but never sends a response? 
Your thread waits.
One thread isn't a problem.
Now imagine:
10,000 concurrent requests
and each waits indefinitely.
You can end up with:
Threads / connections occupied
        ↓
less capacity
        ↓
requests wait longer
        ↓
more resources occupied
        ↓
system becomes slower
        ↓
eventually collapse
So timeout isn't merely:
"Don't wait too long."
It is a resource-protection mechanism.
That's the first important mental model.
________________________________________
2. Naive solution → "Just set a timeout"
An engineer might say:
client.setTimeout(5 seconds);
Looks simple.
But what does "5 seconds" actually mean?
Consider an HTTP request.
There are multiple stages:
Client
  |
  | DNS lookup
  |
  | TCP connection
  |
  | TLS handshake
  |
  | send request
  |
  | wait for response
  |
  | receive response
  v
Server
Where exactly should the five seconds apply?
This is why production networking libraries expose different timeout concepts.
________________________________________
3. Connection Timeout
Suppose:
OrderService
     |
     | TCP connection
     X
PaymentService
The client is trying to establish a connection.
Maybe:
PaymentService
is unreachable.
Or the network path is broken.
Or the server isn't accepting connections quickly enough.
The connection timeout limits how long we're willing to wait to establish the connection.
Conceptually:
connect()
   |
   |------ waiting ------|
                         |
                      timeout
For example:
connectionTimeout = 500ms
means:
"If I can't establish the connection within this amount of time, stop trying this connection attempt."
________________________________________
4. But connection succeeded
Now suppose:
OrderService
     |
     | TCP connection succeeds
     v
PaymentService
So connection timeout is no longer relevant.
Then we send:
POST /payment
and the server does:
receive request
     |
     v
query database
     |
     v
wait...
     |
     v
wait...
Meanwhile:
OrderService
     |
     | waiting for response
     |
     |
     |
     X
This is where read/response timeout becomes important.
________________________________________
5. Read Timeout
A read timeout is concerned with waiting for data from an established connection.
Conceptually:
TCP connection established
        |
        v
send request
        |
        v
wait for response bytes
        |
        |..............|
                     timeout
The important distinction:
Connection timeout
    ↓
Could I establish communication?

Read timeout
    ↓
After communication was established,
did I receive data within the allowed time?
________________________________________
6. Here's where it gets interesting
Suppose PaymentService starts responding.
You receive:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Then nothing.
OrderService
     |
     | <--- HTTP headers
     |
     | <--- some bytes
     |
     | ........ waiting ........
     |
Depending on the client/library, the relevant read/response timeout semantics may concern waiting for the next data rather than the entire operation.
This is why you cannot blindly interpret:
readTimeout = 2 seconds
as:
"The whole HTTP request must finish in two seconds."
That may be completely wrong.
________________________________________
7. Request Timeout / Overall Timeout
Now we introduce the concept you really need for distributed systems:
How long are we willing to spend on the entire operation?
Imagine:
DNS       50ms
connect  100ms
TLS       80ms
write     20ms
server   800ms
read     200ms
----------------
total   1.25 sec
A request/overall timeout might say:
maximum operation duration = 2 seconds
So regardless of where the time is spent:
t = 0
   |
   +-- DNS
   +-- connect
   +-- TLS
   +-- request
   +-- server processing
   +-- response
   |
t = 2 sec
   |
   X timeout
This is much closer to the concept of a deadline.
We'll make that distinction very important in Lesson 2.
________________________________________
8. The three mental models
Don't memorize library-specific terminology because different Java libraries use slightly different names and semantics.
Instead remember:
CONNECT
   ↓
Can I establish the connection?

READ / RESPONSE
   ↓
Once connected, am I receiving response data?

REQUEST / DEADLINE
   ↓
How long can the entire logical operation take?
That mental model survives changes in:
•	Java HTTP client 
•	Apache HttpClient 
•	OkHttp 
•	Netty 
•	gRPC 
•	cloud SDKs 
